  "body": "readRegMem32() and writeRegMem32() count len in 32-bit words, but setDCDCworkaround() and resetDCDCworkaround() passed sizeof(uint32_t). The read wrote 12 bytes of stack past adcCtrlRaw, and each write sent 12 bytes of adjacent stack to the registers after DCDC_FREQ_LF. Pass 1, as Semtech's reference implementation does. Regression from #1864.\r\n\r\nCloses https://github.com/jgromes/RadioLib/issues/1879\n\n\n---\n\n**Addendum: second commit, `LRxxxx::writeCommon()` sends one byte too many on LR2021**\n\nFound while testing the fix above against a recording HAL. `writeCommon()` sizes its buffer as `4 + 4*len` and sends all of it, but since e3af85867 (\"[LR2021] Fix address widths\", first in 7.6.0) it writes only a 3-byte address when the address width is below 32 bits. So every LR2021 `writeRegMem32()` sends one uninitialised trailing byte: 10 bytes where 9 are meant for a single word. LR11x0 isn't affected (32-bit address).\n\n94755e9ca works out the address length once and sizes the buffer from it, so the allocation and the bytes sent both match what was written. With both commits, the DC-DC write to `DCDC_FREQ_LF` goes out as opcode + 3-byte address + one word.\n\nBefore 7.8.0 nothing inside RadioLib called LR2021's `writeRegMem32()`; the DC-DC workaround from #1864 is the first internal caller, so on 7.8.0 this runs on every `setRxPath()` and LoRa modulation change.\n",
